WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.702
The central quantum concern for TLS planning is that some widely used

00:00:04.702 --> 00:00:08.920
public-key algorithms are considered vulnerable to future cryptographically relevant quantum computers.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.837
RSA, finite-field Diffie-Hellman, elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman,

00:00:10.837 --> 00:00:14.288
and ECDSA appear across many web and service environments.

00:00:14.288 --> 00:00:18.506
This does not mean every TLS endpoint is instantly unsafe today.

00:00:18.506 --> 00:00:23.108
It means teams should identify where these mechanisms protect information that must

00:00:23.108 --> 00:00:26.176
remain trustworthy or confidential over a long period.

00:00:26.176 --> 00:00:28.860
Symmetric encryption and hashing are affected differently.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.928
They are still part of quantum risk discussions,

00:00:31.928 --> 00:00:36.146
but the migration pressure is not identical to the public-key case.

00:00:36.146 --> 00:00:36.913
In TLS,

00:00:36.913 --> 00:00:41.515
the public-key portion of the handshake is what makes captured traffic and

00:00:41.515 --> 00:00:43.432
future decryption scenarios especially important.

00:00:43.432 --> 00:00:46.500
The application data is protected with symmetric keys,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:52.252
but the way those keys were established may become the weak link for long-lived confidentiality.

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:55.703
Prioritization should consider data lifetime, confidentiality sensitivity, internet exposure,

00:00:55.703 --> 00:00:59.154
operational criticality, dependency complexity, and business or regulatory impact.

00:00:59.154 --> 00:01:02.222
Public-facing endpoints carrying sensitive data usually deserve attention.

00:01:02.222 --> 00:01:06.057
So do government and enterprise APIs, machine-to-machine services, mobile backends,

00:01:06.057 --> 00:01:09.124
internal service mesh traffic with sensitive business data,

00:01:09.124 --> 00:01:11.809
and partner integrations that cross organizational boundaries.

00:01:11.809 --> 00:01:17.561
A static brochure site has a different risk profile than an API carrying regulated records.

00:01:17.561 --> 00:01:22.162
Not every endpoint has the same urgency, and that is good news.

00:01:22.162 --> 00:01:27.531
A mature program can rank systems instead of trying to change everything at once.

00:01:27.531 --> 00:01:31.366
Teams should identify high-value traffic, long-lived data, externally exposed services,

00:01:31.366 --> 00:01:36.734
clients that are hard to update, and dependencies controlled by vendors or cloud providers.

00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:41.336
Prioritization turns PQC from a vague concern into a sequenced roadmap that

00:01:41.336 --> 00:01:44.020
engineering and operations teams can actually execute.
